import{_ as s,o as t,c as a,ao as i}from"./chunks/framework.D26l3Sq6.js";const g=JSON.parse('{"title":"Extending the bindings: wrapping a new VTK class","description":"","frontmatter":{},"headers":[],"relativePath":"implementation/extending.md","filePath":"implementation/extending.md","lastUpdated":null}'),n={name:"implementation/extending.md"};function l(o,e,r,d,p,h){return t(),a("div",null,[...e[0]||(e[0]=[i(`<h1 id="Extending-the-bindings:-wrapping-a-new-VTK-class" tabindex="-1">Extending the bindings: wrapping a new VTK class <a class="header-anchor" href="#Extending-the-bindings:-wrapping-a-new-VTK-class" aria-label="Permalink to &quot;Extending the bindings: wrapping a new VTK class {#Extending-the-bindings:-wrapping-a-new-VTK-class}&quot;">​</a></h1><p>This guide walks through adding a VTK class to VTK.jl <strong>by hand</strong> — no AI required. It covers the three layers of the pipeline (see <a href="./architecture">Architecture</a> and the ELI5 in the repo root) and the build/test cycle. We use a concrete example: wrapping <code>vtkGradientFilter</code>, a CFD-useful filter we have not bound yet.</p><h2 id="The-pipeline-in-one-picture" tabindex="-1">The pipeline in one picture <a class="header-anchor" href="#The-pipeline-in-one-picture" aria-label="Permalink to &quot;The pipeline in one picture {#The-pipeline-in-one-picture}&quot;">​</a></h2><div class="language- vp-adaptive-theme"><button title="Copy Code" class="copy"></button><span class="lang"></span><pre class="shiki shiki-themes github-light github-dark vp-code" tabindex="0"><code><span class="line"><span>VTK headers (VTK_jll)                what you edit</span></span>
<span class="line"><span>        │                            ┌──────────────────────────────┐</span></span>
<span class="line"><span>        ▼                            │ 1. gen/wrapit/VTK.wit.in │</span></span>
<span class="line"><span>   ┌──────────┐  generates  ┌──────────────┐  merges  ┌────────────────┐</span></span>
<span class="line"><span>   │  WrapIt  │ ──────────► │ JlClasses.cxx│ ◄─────── │ shim_vtkjl.cpp │</span></span>
<span class="line"><span>   │ upstream │   ~95%      │  (generated) │          │  (hand-written)│</span></span>
<span class="line"><span>   └──────────┘             └──────────────┘          └────────────────┘</span></span>
<span class="line"><span>        │                            │                      auto_shim.jl</span></span>
<span class="line"><span>        ▼                            ▼                    (generated shim)</span></span>
<span class="line"><span>   one libvtk_julia.so  ◄── 2. gen/build_wrapper.jl</span></span>
<span class="line"><span>        │</span></span>
<span class="line"><span>        ▼                            ┌──────────────────────────────┐</span></span>
<span class="line"><span>   @wrapmodule at \`using VTK\`  ◄─── │ 3. src/*.jl (Julian layer)   │</span></span>
<span class="line"><span>                                    └──────────────────────────────┘</span></span></code></pre></div><p><strong>Rule of thumb:</strong> WrapIt handles the boring 95% (type registration, plain methods). You only hand-write what a generator cannot infer: ownership policy, macro-generated API that is invisible to the C++ AST, zero-copy tricks, and ergonomic adapters.</p><h2 id="Step-0-—-prerequisites" tabindex="-1">Step 0 — prerequisites <a class="header-anchor" href="#Step-0-—-prerequisites" aria-label="Permalink to &quot;Step 0 — prerequisites {#Step-0-—-prerequisites}&quot;">​</a></h2><p>Same as the normal build (see the README): git, CMake, a C++17 compiler, libclang + OpenSSL dev headers, and the Julia project instantiated. You do <strong>not</strong> need a local VTK build — headers and libraries come from <code>VTK_jll</code>.</p><h2 id="Step-1-—-tell-WrapIt-about-the-class" tabindex="-1">Step 1 — tell WrapIt about the class <a class="header-anchor" href="#Step-1-—-tell-WrapIt-about-the-class" aria-label="Permalink to &quot;Step 1 — tell WrapIt about the class {#Step-1-—-tell-WrapIt-about-the-class}&quot;">​</a></h2><p>Add the class&#39;s header to the <code>input</code> list in <code>gen/wrapit/VTK.wit.in</code>:</p><div class="language-toml vp-adaptive-theme"><button title="Copy Code" class="copy"></button><span class="lang">toml</span><pre class="shiki shiki-themes github-light github-dark vp-code" tabindex="0"><code><span class="line"><span style="--shiki-light:#24292E;--shiki-dark:#E1E4E8;">input = [</span></span>
<span class="line"><span style="--shiki-light:#B31D28;--shiki-light-font-style:italic;--shiki-dark:#FDAEB7;--shiki-dark-font-style:italic;">  ...</span></span>
<span class="line"><span style="--shiki-light:#032F62;--shiki-dark:#9ECBFF;">  &quot;vtkDataSetAlgorithm.h&quot;</span><span style="--shiki-light:#24292E;--shiki-dark:#E1E4E8;">,</span></span>
<span class="line"><span style="--shiki-light:#032F62;--shiki-dark:#9ECBFF;">  &quot;vtkGradientFilter.h&quot;</span><span style="--shiki-light:#24292E;--shiki-dark:#E1E4E8;">,   </span><span style="--shiki-light:#6A737D;--shiki-dark:#6A737D;"># &lt;-- new</span></span>
<span class="line"><span style="--shiki-light:#24292E;--shiki-dark:#E1E4E8;">]</span></span></code></pre></div><p>Notes:</p><ul><li><p><strong>Include the full inheritance chain</strong> if the new class&#39;s bases are not already listed. WrapIt registers a class under its direct base with <code>julia_base_type&lt;...&gt;</code>; if the base is missing you get a compile error naming it — just add that header too.</p></li><li><p>If WrapIt drops a <em>template</em> base (e.g. <code>vtkStructuredData</code>, or <code>vtkAOSDataArrayTemplate&lt;T&gt;</code> for the leaf arrays), the driver re-points the class in <code>postprocess!()</code> in <code>gen/wrapit/build.jl</code> — follow the existing <code>vtkStructuredGrid</code> / <code>AOS_LEAF_ARRAYS</code> examples there.</p></li><li><p>Methods you do <strong>not</strong> want can be vetoed: add their <code>// signature to use in the veto list:</code> line from the generated <code>JlClasses.cxx</code> to <code>gen/wrapit/veto.txt</code>.</p></li></ul><h2 id="Step-2-—-build-and-inspect" tabindex="-1">Step 2 — build and inspect <a class="header-anchor" href="#Step-2-—-build-and-inspect" aria-label="Permalink to &quot;Step 2 — build and inspect {#Step-2-—-build-and-inspect}&quot;">​</a></h2><div class="language-sh vp-adaptive-theme"><button title="Copy Code" class="copy"></button><span class="lang">sh</span><pre class="shiki shiki-themes github-light github-dark vp-code" tabindex="0"><code><span class="line"><span style="--shiki-light:#6F42C1;--shiki-dark:#B392F0;">julia</span><span style="--shiki-light:#005CC5;--shiki-dark:#79B8FF;"> --project=.</span><span style="--shiki-light:#032F62;--shiki-dark:#9ECBFF;"> gen/build_wrapper.jl</span></span></code></pre></div><p>The driver: renders the config → builds/runs wrapit → generates the auto-shim → self-heals shim/generator clashes via <code>veto_shim_clashes!()</code> (watch for &quot;added N shim-clash vetoes; REGENERATE&quot; — it reruns itself and converges) → post-processes → compiles one <code>libvtk_julia.so</code> and installs it at <code>wrapper/build/</code>.</p><p>Then inspect what you got (this is the key skill — read the generated code):</p><div class="language-sh vp-adaptive-theme"><button title="Copy Code" class="copy"></button><span class="lang">sh</span><pre class="shiki shiki-themes github-light github-dark vp-code" tabindex="0"><code><span class="line"><span style="--shiki-light:#6A737D;--shiki-dark:#6A737D;"># Did the class get registered? With which base?</span></span>
<span class="line"><span style="--shiki-light:#6F42C1;--shiki-dark:#B392F0;">grep</span><span style="--shiki-light:#005CC5;--shiki-dark:#79B8FF;"> -n</span><span style="--shiki-light:#032F62;--shiki-dark:#9ECBFF;"> &#39;add_type&lt;vtkGradientFilter&gt;&#39;</span><span style="--shiki-light:#032F62;--shiki-dark:#9ECBFF;"> gen/wrapit/build/cxx/JlClasses.cxx</span></span>
<span class="line"></span>
<span class="line"><span style="--shiki-light:#6A737D;--shiki-dark:#6A737D;"># Which methods came along?</span></span>
<span class="line"><span style="--shiki-light:#6F42C1;--shiki-dark:#B392F0;">grep</span><span style="--shiki-light:#005CC5;--shiki-dark:#79B8FF;"> -n</span><span style="--shiki-light:#032F62;--shiki-dark:#9ECBFF;"> &#39;method(&quot;&#39;</span><span style="--shiki-light:#032F62;--shiki-dark:#9ECBFF;"> gen/wrapit/build/cxx/JlClasses.cxx</span><span style="--shiki-light:#D73A49;--shiki-dark:#F97583;"> |</span><span style="--shiki-light:#6F42C1;--shiki-dark:#B392F0;"> grep</span><span style="--shiki-light:#005CC5;--shiki-dark:#79B8FF;"> -i</span><span style="--shiki-light:#032F62;--shiki-dark:#9ECBFF;"> gradient</span></span></code></pre></div><h2 id="Step-3-—-decide-what-the-shim-must-add" tabindex="-1">Step 3 — decide what the shim must add <a class="header-anchor" href="#Step-3-—-decide-what-the-shim-must-add" aria-label="Permalink to &quot;Step 3 — decide what the shim must add {#Step-3-—-decide-what-the-shim-must-add}&quot;">​</a></h2><p>Ask three questions:</p><ol><li><p><strong>Does the class have macro-generated setters/getters or a <code>New()</code> factory?</strong> The auto-shim (<code>auto_shim.jl</code>) already emits <code>X_new</code> and <code>SetFoo</code>/<code>GetFoo</code> for <code>vtkSetMacro</code>/<code>vtkGetMacro</code>/string/boolean/vector macros — check <code>build/cxx/shim_auto.cpp</code>. If it is there, nothing to do.</p></li><li><p><strong>Does any method return a pointer whose ownership Julia must manage?</strong> Factories and <code>GetOutput()</code> → return <code>vtkSmartPointer&lt;T&gt;</code> (see <code>X_new</code> in the shim). Borrowed getters → <code>take_ref&lt;T&gt;(raw)</code> (see <code>GetPointData</code>). Getting this wrong = crashes or leaks.</p></li><li><p><strong>Is the raw API ergonomic enough?</strong> VTK often takes <code>(i, x, y, z)</code> or fills an output argument; Julia wants tuples and returned values. Add a convenience overload in the shim (see <code>GetPoint</code> returning a tuple).</p></li></ol><p>For <code>vtkGradientFilter</code>, the auto-shim gives us <code>GradientFilter_new</code> and the macro setters (<code>SetComputeGradient</code>, …) for free. We only add a convenience wrapper if we want nicer argument order — optional.</p><h2 id="Step-4-—-Julian-layer" tabindex="-1">Step 4 — Julian layer <a class="header-anchor" href="#Step-4-—-Julian-layer" aria-label="Permalink to &quot;Step 4 — Julian layer {#Step-4-—-Julian-layer}&quot;">​</a></h2><p>Add the friendly function to <code>src/filters.jl</code> (or <code>dataset.jl</code> for I/O):</p><div class="language-julia vp-adaptive-theme"><button title="Copy Code" class="copy"></button><span class="lang">julia</span><pre class="shiki shiki-themes github-light github-dark vp-code" tabindex="0"><code><span class="line"><span style="--shiki-light:#032F62;--shiki-dark:#9ECBFF;">&quot;&quot;&quot;</span></span>
<span class="line"><span style="--shiki-light:#032F62;--shiki-dark:#9ECBFF;">    gradient(grid; scalars) -&gt; vtkUnstructuredGrid</span></span>
<span class="line"><span style="--shiki-light:#032F62;--shiki-dark:#9ECBFF;">&quot;&quot;&quot;</span></span>
<span class="line"><span style="--shiki-light:#D73A49;--shiki-dark:#F97583;">function</span><span style="--shiki-light:#6F42C1;--shiki-dark:#B392F0;"> gradient</span><span style="--shiki-light:#24292E;--shiki-dark:#E1E4E8;">(grid; scalars </span><span style="--shiki-light:#D73A49;--shiki-dark:#F97583;">=</span><span style="--shiki-light:#005CC5;--shiki-dark:#79B8FF;"> nothing</span><span style="--shiki-light:#24292E;--shiki-dark:#E1E4E8;">)</span></span>
<span class="line"><span style="--shiki-light:#24292E;--shiki-dark:#E1E4E8;">    f </span><span style="--shiki-light:#D73A49;--shiki-dark:#F97583;">=</span><span style="--shiki-light:#005CC5;--shiki-dark:#79B8FF;"> GradientFilter_new</span><span style="--shiki-light:#24292E;--shiki-dark:#E1E4E8;">()</span></span>
<span class="line"><span style="--shiki-light:#005CC5;--shiki-dark:#79B8FF;">    SetInputData</span><span style="--shiki-light:#24292E;--shiki-dark:#E1E4E8;">(f, grid)</span></span>
<span class="line"><span style="--shiki-light:#24292E;--shiki-dark:#E1E4E8;">    scalars </span><span style="--shiki-light:#D73A49;--shiki-dark:#F97583;">===</span><span style="--shiki-light:#005CC5;--shiki-dark:#79B8FF;"> nothing</span><span style="--shiki-light:#D73A49;--shiki-dark:#F97583;"> ||</span><span style="--shiki-light:#005CC5;--shiki-dark:#79B8FF;"> SetInputScalars</span><span style="--shiki-light:#24292E;--shiki-dark:#E1E4E8;">(f, </span><span style="--shiki-light:#005CC5;--shiki-dark:#79B8FF;">0</span><span style="--shiki-light:#24292E;--shiki-dark:#E1E4E8;">, </span><span style="--shiki-light:#005CC5;--shiki-dark:#79B8FF;">String</span><span style="--shiki-light:#24292E;--shiki-dark:#E1E4E8;">(scalars))  </span><span style="--shiki-light:#6A737D;--shiki-dark:#6A737D;"># check the generated name</span></span>
<span class="line"><span style="--shiki-light:#005CC5;--shiki-dark:#79B8FF;">    update!</span><span style="--shiki-light:#24292E;--shiki-dark:#E1E4E8;">(f)</span></span>
<span class="line"><span style="--shiki-light:#D73A49;--shiki-dark:#F97583;">    return</span><span style="--shiki-light:#005CC5;--shiki-dark:#79B8FF;"> UnstructuredGrid</span><span style="--shiki-light:#24292E;--shiki-dark:#E1E4E8;">(</span><span style="--shiki-light:#005CC5;--shiki-dark:#79B8FF;">GetOutput</span><span style="--shiki-light:#24292E;--shiki-dark:#E1E4E8;">(f))</span></span>
<span class="line"><span style="--shiki-light:#D73A49;--shiki-dark:#F97583;">end</span></span></code></pre></div><p>Idioms you will reuse constantly:</p><ul><li><p>Call wrapped methods directly with smart pointers. The generated overloads in <code>src/generated_methods.jl</code> insert the required <code>_cp(vtkBaseClass, obj)</code> conversion automatically; raw-pointer calls with explicit <code>_cp</code> remain valid.</p></li><li><p><code>update!(algo)</code> — calls <code>Update</code> and throws if VTK reported an error.</p></li><li><p><code>UnstructuredGrid(GetOutput(...))</code> — downcast the generic output to the concrete type. The generic <code>downcast(obj)</code> dispatches on <code>GetClassName()</code> through <code>DOWNCAST_REGISTRY</code>, which is built automatically at module load from the registered types + their <code>downcast_foo</code> shim bindings — add your type to <code>DOWNCAST_SOURCES</code> in <code>src/dataset.jl</code> only if it is a new kind of grid (the existing entries cover all current ones).</p></li></ul><h2 id="Step-5-—-test" tabindex="-1">Step 5 — test <a class="header-anchor" href="#Step-5-—-test" aria-label="Permalink to &quot;Step 5 — test {#Step-5-—-test}&quot;">​</a></h2><p>Add a testset to <code>test/filters.jl</code> (or a new file + <code>include</code> in <code>test/runtests.jl</code>). Two kinds of checks, cheapest first:</p><ol><li><p><strong>Smoke</strong>: the call runs, output has expected type/point count.</p></li><li><p><strong>Oracle</strong>: verify the numbers against an analytic or independent result — e.g. gradient of a linear field is constant. For I/O, cross-check against ReadVTK.jl/WriteVTK.jl (see <code>test/oracle.jl</code>).</p></li></ol><p>Then:</p><div class="language-sh vp-adaptive-theme"><button title="Copy Code" class="copy"></button><span class="lang">sh</span><pre class="shiki shiki-themes github-light github-dark vp-code" tabindex="0"><code><span class="line"><span style="--shiki-light:#6F42C1;--shiki-dark:#B392F0;">rm</span><span style="--shiki-light:#005CC5;--shiki-dark:#79B8FF;"> -rf</span><span style="--shiki-light:#032F62;--shiki-dark:#9ECBFF;"> ~/.julia/compiled/v1.12/VTK</span><span style="--shiki-light:#6A737D;--shiki-dark:#6A737D;">   # precompile cache ignores VTKJL_LIB!</span></span>
<span class="line"><span style="--shiki-light:#6F42C1;--shiki-dark:#B392F0;">julia</span><span style="--shiki-light:#005CC5;--shiki-dark:#79B8FF;"> --project=test</span><span style="--shiki-light:#032F62;--shiki-dark:#9ECBFF;"> test/runtests.jl</span></span></code></pre></div><p>Expect the full suite green (count grows by your new tests). Also verify the hand-written fallback still builds and passes — it lags the merged lib, so new generated-only tests must self-skip:</p><div class="language-text vp-adaptive-theme"><button title="Copy Code" class="copy"></button><span class="lang">text</span><pre class="shiki shiki-themes github-light github-dark vp-code" tabindex="0"><code><span class="line"><span>@testset &quot;my new feature&quot; begin</span></span>
<span class="line"><span>    if !isdefined(VTK, :GradientFilter_new)</span></span>
<span class="line"><span>        @info &quot;not present (hand-written wrapper) — skipping&quot;</span></span>
<span class="line"><span>        return</span></span>
<span class="line"><span>    end</span></span>
<span class="line"><span>    ...</span></span>
<span class="line"><span>end</span></span></code></pre></div><h2 id="Troubleshooting" tabindex="-1">Troubleshooting <a class="header-anchor" href="#Troubleshooting" aria-label="Permalink to &quot;Troubleshooting {#Troubleshooting}&quot;">​</a></h2><table tabindex="0"><thead><tr><th style="text-align:right;">Symptom</th><th style="text-align:right;">Cause / fix</th></tr></thead><tbody><tr><td style="text-align:right;"><code>shim references types the generated module does not register: vtkX</code></td><td style="text-align:right;">Add <code>vtkX.h</code> to the <code>input</code> list (reader/writer headers alone do not register their data types).</td></tr><tr><td style="text-align:right;">C++ compile error: <code>no member named ...</code> in generated code</td><td style="text-align:right;">A method&#39;s parameter type was not registered; add that class&#39;s header, or veto the method.</td></tr><tr><td style="text-align:right;"><code>added N shim-clash vetoes; REGENERATE</code></td><td style="text-align:right;">Normal: the driver found methods both it and the shim emit; it reruns itself. Only investigate if it does not converge.</td></tr><tr><td style="text-align:right;">Julia <code>MethodError</code> for a wrapped method</td><td style="text-align:right;">The generated overload was vetoed, the smart-pointer forwarding file is stale, or an argument has the wrong type. Rebuild and grep <code>JlClasses.cxx</code> for the exact signature.</td></tr><tr><td style="text-align:right;">Tests pass locally but CI fails</td><td style="text-align:right;">CI builds wrapit from source with a different libclang; check the CLANG_PREFIX notes in <code>AGENTS.md</code>.</td></tr><tr><td style="text-align:right;">Wrong library loaded after switching</td><td style="text-align:right;"><code>rm -rf ~/.julia/compiled/v1.12/VTK</code> — the precompile cache does not track which <code>.so</code> was used.</td></tr></tbody></table><h2 id="When-not-to-extend-the-shim" tabindex="-1">When <em>not</em> to extend the shim <a class="header-anchor" href="#When-not-to-extend-the-shim" aria-label="Permalink to &quot;When _not_ to extend the shim {#When-not-to-extend-the-shim}&quot;">​</a></h2><p>If the class is plain C++ (no macros, no ownership subtleties, no convenience needed), <strong>config + Julian layer is enough</strong> — that is the whole point of the generator. The shim is only for what WrapIt cannot express.</p>`,37)])])}const k=s(n,[["render",l]]);export{g as __pageData,k as default};
